#N canvas 20 20 2600 1800 12;
#X text 20 20 g_grid -- the Launchpad's 96 LEDs \, and their sole owner. The same arbiter shape as g_oled: layers with a priority and a time to live \, callers pushing SEMANTICS onto the disp bus whenever they have something to say \, and a frame clock that redraws whichever layer is winning. Build the pattern once \, instantiate it twice., f 58;
#X text 20 170 creation arg 1: the Pd MIDI OUTPUT PORT the Launchpad is on -- 1 on the Organelle. inlet 0: surface ownership from m_launchpad \, 1 while we are in Programmer Mode. inlet 1: the beat number within the bar \, from the c_clock instance in u_root. outlets: none., f 58;
#X text 20 320 ONE DEVIATION FROM g_oled \, AND IT IS THE INTERESTING ONE. Whole-surface arbitration is right for a 128x64 screen and wrong for a grid \, where the natural idiom is REGIONS. So home is a COMPOSITE -- the mode lights along the top and the beat row along the bottom \, because both belong to normal operation -- while modal and alert take the whole surface. The cascade is unchanged \; the composition happens inside the layer that never expires., f 58;
#X text 20 520 WHO OWNS WHAT \, because two files send bytes to this device. g_grid owns the LEDs \; m_launchpad owns the MODE and the layout. Those are different surfaces and each has exactly one writer \, which is the rule. m_launchpad lights nothing at all: the first frame after ownership is raised paints all 99 indices \, so the mandatory init clear IS that frame and there is no separate clear to keep in step., f 58;
#X obj 20 720 inlet;
#X obj 400 720 inlet;
#X obj 20 780 change -1;
#X obj 20 830 t f f;
#X obj 20 890 s \$0-own;
#X obj 200 890 select 1;
#X msg 200 940 1;
#X obj 200 990 s \$0-dirty;
#X obj 400 780 t f f;
#X obj 400 830 s \$0-beat;
#X msg 560 830 1;
#X obj 560 890 s \$0-dirty;
#X text 760 720 OWNERSHIP GOES THROUGH change -1 \, so re-asserting it costs nothing \, and a 0 to 1 transition sets the dirty flag -- because while we did not own the surface the device was redrawing it and whatever we last sent is gone. THE -1 IS LOAD-BEARING for the same reason it is in u_level and m_organelle: a bare change starts life holding 0 and would swallow a real 0., f 50;
#X obj 20 1080 array define \$0-surface 109;
#X text 20 1140 THE FRAME BUFFER \, one cell per Programmer-Mode index. Only 10 to 108 are ever used -- the paint subpatch says why that span and not the whole surface., f 58;
#N canvas 30 30 1900 1000 disp-in 0;
#X text 20 20 g_grid RIDES THE disp BUS under ONE reserved selector \, grid \, and routes its own vocabulary underneath. That costs two lines in g_oled -- grid appended to its route and the reject moved along -- exactly what led cost in Phase 5 \, and it keeps every display request for every surface in one log stamped with one frame number., f 58;
#X text 20 150 NO list append BEFORE THE SECOND ROUTE. grid modal 45 arrives out of the first route with modal already AS the selector \, which is the shape the second route wants. Same fact as g_led \, and a list append here would break every match., f 58;
#X obj 20 320 r disp;
#X obj 20 380 t a a;
#X obj 20 440 route grid;
#X obj 20 500 route modal modal-off;
#X obj 20 560 t f f;
#X obj 20 620 s \$0-modal-col;
#X msg 200 620 1;
#X obj 200 680 s \$0-on-modal;
#X obj 460 560 t b;
#X msg 460 620 1;
#X obj 460 680 s \$0-off-modal;
#X obj 1000 440 route alert;
#X obj 1000 500 route fail;
#X obj 1000 560 t b;
#X msg 1000 620 1;
#X obj 1000 680 s \$0-on-alert;
#X text 1000 760 ALERT IS READ STRAIGHT OFF disp \, AND ONLY fail. Two consumers of one selector is fine -- the rule is one owner per SURFACE \, not per selector -- so u_err needs no change at all. The grid is visible from much further away than the OLED \, which makes a failure turning the whole surface red the most valuable thing these pads can say in a venue. A warn is not worth the surface., f 50;
#X text 20 800 MODAL CARRIES A PALETTE INDEX \, not a word: grid modal 45 turns everything blue. A caller that wants text puts it on the OLED \; this surface has none \, and pretending otherwise would consume all of it., f 58;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 3 1 4 0;
#X connect 3 0 13 0;
#X connect 4 0 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 5 1 10 0;
#X connect 6 1 7 0;
#X connect 6 0 8 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X connect 10 0 11 0;
#X connect 11 0 12 0;
#X connect 13 0 14 0;
#X connect 14 0 15 0;
#X connect 15 0 16 0;
#X connect 16 0 17 0;
#X restore 20 1260 pd disp-in;
#X text 280 1294 the grid vocabulary \, and the alert it shares with the OLED, f 50;
#N canvas 30 30 1700 900 mode-in 0;
#X text 20 20 THE mode BUS IS READ DIRECTLY \, not through disp. mode is already a bus \, so one send from u_map reaches every surface and each one decides what a mode LOOKS like. This file turns it into a lamp number \, 1 to 6., f 58;
#X obj 20 200 r mode;
#X obj 20 260 route compose perform;
#X obj 20 340 route mode-1 mode-2 mode-3 mode-4 mode-5 mode-6;
#X msg 20 420 1;
#X msg 120 420 2;
#X msg 220 420 3;
#X msg 320 420 4;
#X msg 420 420 5;
#X msg 520 420 6;
#X obj 20 500 t f f;
#X obj 20 560 s \$0-mode;
#X msg 200 560 1;
#X obj 200 620 s \$0-dirty;
#X text 20 700 TWO ROUTES AND NO list append BETWEEN THEM. mode carries class then sub-mode -- compose mode-1 -- so the remainder out of the first route is mode-1 already AS a selector. THE CLASS IS DELIBERATELY DISCARDED HERE: u_err is what cares whether we are in compose or perform \, and what this surface shows is WHICH of the six is live., f 58;
#X text 900 200 A mode this file does not recognise falls out of the second route's reject and changes nothing \, which is the right failure: the grid keeps showing the last mode it understood rather than going blank or inventing one., f 50;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 2 1 3 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 3 1 5 0;
#X connect 3 2 6 0;
#X connect 3 3 7 0;
#X connect 3 4 8 0;
#X connect 3 5 9 0;
#X connect 4 0 10 0;
#X connect 5 0 10 0;
#X connect 6 0 10 0;
#X connect 7 0 10 0;
#X connect 8 0 10 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
#X connect 10 1 11 0;
#X connect 10 0 12 0;
#X connect 12 0 13 0;
#X restore 20 1340 pd mode-in;
#X text 280 1374 the mode bus \, finally with a driver \, turned into a lamp number, f 50;
#N canvas 30 30 1600 950 layers 0;
#X text 20 20 ONE delay PER LAYER \, RETRIGGERED -- g_oled's shape exactly. home has no entry here: it is always active and never expires \, it is what the cascade falls through to., f 58;
#X obj 20 180 r \$0-on-modal;
#X obj 20 240 t b b b;
#X msg 200 300 1;
#X obj 20 300 del 30000;
#X msg 20 440 0;
#X obj 20 500 s \$0-a-modal;
#X msg 380 300 1;
#X obj 380 360 s \$0-dirty;
#X obj 760 180 r \$0-on-alert;
#X obj 760 240 t b b b;
#X msg 940 300 1;
#X obj 760 300 del 2000;
#X msg 760 440 0;
#X obj 760 500 s \$0-a-alert;
#X msg 1120 300 1;
#X obj 1120 360 s \$0-dirty;
#X obj 20 540 r \$0-off-modal;
#X obj 20 600 t b b;
#X msg 20 660 0;
#X obj 20 720 s \$0-a-modal;
#X msg 200 660 1;
#X obj 200 720 s \$0-dirty;
#X text 20 800 EVERY RAISE AND EVERY EXPIRY SETS DIRTY \, and that is what makes a timeout visible at all. The flag is not only for incoming state: a layer falling away changes the frame just as much as one arriving \, and without it an expired alert would stay red until something else happened to move., f 58;
#X text 760 540 THE ALERT TTL IS FIXED AT 2 s HERE \, where g_oled varies it by level. This layer only ever sees fail \, so there is one number and no field to carry it., f 50;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 2 3 0;
#X connect 2 1 7 0;
#X connect 2 0 4 0;
#X connect 3 0 6 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
