#X connect 15 0 16 0;
#X connect 17 0 18 0;
#X connect 18 1 19 0;
#X connect 18 0 21 0;
#X connect 19 0 20 0;
#X connect 21 0 22 0;
#X obj 20 360 t b b;
#X msg 200 440 1;
#X obj 200 500 s \$0-dirty;
#X obj 760 360 t b b;
#X msg 940 440 1;
#X obj 940 500 s \$0-dirty;
#X connect 4 0 25 0;
#X connect 25 1 5 0;
#X connect 25 0 26 0;
#X connect 26 0 27 0;
#X connect 12 0 28 0;
#X connect 28 1 13 0;
#X connect 28 0 29 0;
#X connect 29 0 30 0;
#X restore 20 1420 pd layers;
#X text 280 1454 the active flag and the time to live for each layer, f 50;
#N canvas 30 30 1300 700 pick 0;
#X text 20 20 PRIORITY AS A CASCADE \, NOT ARITHMETIC -- copied from g_oled's pick \, one layer shorter. select 1 passes the non-matching 0 out of its right outlet \, so this reads top to bottom in exactly priority order., f 58;
#X obj 20 160 inlet;
#X obj 20 220 f;
#X obj 160 180 r \$0-a-alert;
#X obj 20 280 select 1;
#X obj 20 340 outlet;
#X obj 360 220 f;
#X obj 500 180 r \$0-a-modal;
#X obj 360 280 select 1;
#X obj 360 340 outlet;
#X obj 240 340 t b;
#X obj 620 340 t b;
#X obj 700 400 outlet;
#X text 20 480 Outlet order is x position \, so left to right these are alert \, modal \, home -- highest priority first \, which is also the order they are tested in. f with no argument starts at 0 \, so before anything has fired every layer is down and home wins \, which is the correct cold start., f 58;
#X text 700 480 The t b is not decoration: select's right outlet emits the float it did NOT match \, and f wants a bang on its hot inlet -- a 0 would overwrite the flag it is about to read., f 46;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 3 0 2 1;
#X connect 2 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 0 5 0;
#X connect 4 1 10 0;
#X connect 10 0 6 0;
#X connect 7 0 6 1;
#X connect 6 0 8 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X connect 8 1 11 0;
#X connect 11 0 12 0;
#X restore 700 1500 pd pick;
#X text 960 1534 which layer wins this frame, f 50;
#N canvas 30 30 2000 1100 prepare 0;
#X text 20 20 FILLS THE FRAME BUFFER \, AND EMITS NOTHING. One inlet per layer \, wired from pick. Whichever fires \, the whole span is filled first -- so nothing from the previous frame can survive -- and only then does home draw its regions on top., f 58;
#X obj 20 200 inlet;
#X obj 300 200 inlet;
#X obj 620 200 inlet;
#X msg 20 260 5;
#X obj 300 260 f;
#X obj 440 210 r \$0-modal-col;
#X obj 620 260 t b b;
#X msg 620 330 0;
#X obj 20 430 t b b f;
#X obj 20 510 f;
#X msg 300 490 99;
#X obj 300 550 until;
#X obj 300 610 f;
#X obj 460 670 + 1;
#X obj 300 670 + 10;
#X obj 300 730 t b f;
#X obj 300 800 tabwrite \$0-surface;
#X msg 620 490 0;
#N canvas 40 40 1700 950 regions 0;
#X text 20 20 HOME'S REGIONS \, drawn on top of the fill. Each one is DIM EVERYWHERE THEN BRIGHT AT ONE CELL \, which is two short loops rather than a comparison inside one -- fewer objects \, and nothing to get wrong about which cell is current., f 58;
#X obj 20 180 inlet;
#X obj 20 240 t b b b b;
#X obj 20 310 t b b;
#X msg 200 370 0;
#X msg 20 370 6;
#X obj 20 430 until;
#X obj 20 490 f;
#X obj 20 550 t f f;
#X obj 180 610 + 1;
#X obj 20 610 + 91;
#X obj 20 670 t b f;
#X msg 20 740 1;
#X obj 20 800 tabwrite \$0-surface;
#X obj 440 310 t b;
#X obj 440 370 f;
#X obj 580 330 r \$0-mode;
#X obj 440 430 + 90;
#X obj 440 490 t b f;
#X msg 440 560 21;
#X obj 440 620 tabwrite \$0-surface;
#X obj 860 310 t b b;
#X msg 1040 370 0;
#X msg 860 370 8;
#X obj 860 430 until;
#X obj 860 490 f;
#X obj 860 550 t f f;
#X obj 1020 610 + 1;
#X obj 860 610 + 11;
#X obj 860 670 t b f;
#X msg 860 740 1;
#X obj 860 800 tabwrite \$0-surface;
#X obj 1280 310 t b;
#X obj 1280 370 f;
#X obj 1420 330 r \$0-beat;
#X obj 1280 430 + 10;
#X obj 1280 490 t b f;
#X msg 1280 560 3;
#X obj 1280 620 tabwrite \$0-surface;
#X text 20 880 THE MODE ROW IS CC 91 TO 96 \, six of the eight along the top \, so all 64 pads stay free. Those numbers are MEASURED on this unit rather than taken from the documentation -- which turned out to be missing a whole row of buttons and the corner at 90., f 58;
#X text 860 880 THE BEAT ROW IS GRID ROW 1 \, indices 11 to 18 \, the BOTTOM row of pads -- row 1 at the bottom is the device's own numbering and it happens to read the right way round for a playhead. THE TWO BRANCHES ADD DIFFERENT NUMBERS AND THAT IS NOT A TYPO: the dim loop counts 0 to 7 so it adds 11 \, while c_clock's beat number is ONE-BASED \, 1 to 8 \, so the bright one adds 10. MEASURED \, not assumed -- with 11 on both \, beat 8 landed on index 19 \, lighting a ring button and blanking the row once per bar. Seven beats out of eight looked perfect., f 58;
#X text 1280 700 tabwrite TAKES THE VALUE ON THE LEFT AND THE INDEX ON THE RIGHT \, measured rather than assumed \, so every one of these is t b f: the index first into the cold inlet \, then the colour., f 46;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 3 3 0;
#X connect 2 2 14 0;
#X connect 2 1 21 0;
#X connect 2 0 32 0;
#X connect 3 1 4 0;
#X connect 3 0 5 0;
